# 🛠️ Lab Setup Steps

This file contains a full breakdown of the steps used to build a practical cybersecurity lab focused on Active Directory, user access control, and Group Policy management in a virtualized environment.

---

## 🔧 1. Virtual Machine Creation

- Installed **VirtualBox** on host machine (Windows 10)
- Downloaded and mounted:
  - **Windows Server 2019 ISO**
  - **Windows 10 ISO**
- Created two VMs:
  - `LABDC01` (Server 2019 – Domain Controller)
  - `HR-PC01` (Windows 10 client)
- Cloned HR-PC01 to create:
  - `IT-PC01`, `Finance-PC01`, `Sales-PC01`

---

## 🌐 2. Network Configuration

- Configured **Host-Only Adapter** in VirtualBox
- Set static IPs: Domain Controller: 192.168.56.10 (also DNS)
Clients: .11, .12, .13, .14 (per machine)
Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
Gateway: 192.168.56.1 (VirtualBox host-only)

- Verified connectivity with `ping`
- Confirmed domain controller responds to `nslookup lab.local`

---

## 🧱 3. Active Directory Setup

- Installed **Active Directory Domain Services** and **DNS** on `LABDC01`
- Promoted server to domain controller with:
  - Domain Name: `lab.local`
  - NetBIOS Name: `LAB`
- Verified domain setup through Server Manager and Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC)

---

## 🏗️ 4. Organizational Unit (OU) Design

- Created top-level OUs:
  - `Company`
  - `Security Groups`
  - `Workstations`
- Under `Company`, created:
  - `HR`, `IT`, `Finance`, `Sales`
- Under `Workstations`, created:
  - `HR-Computers`, `IT-Computers`, `Finance-Computers`, `Sales-Computers`

---

## 👤 5. User & Group Creation

- Created domain users per department:
  - `SarahB`, `KarenL`, `DanielM`, `EmilyR`, `JasonT`, `EvanC`, `MarcusJ`, `LaurenP`
- Created Security Groups:
  - `hr-staff`, `it-admins`, `finance-staff`, `sales-staff`
  - `vpn-users`, `remote-desktop-users`
- Assigned users to appropriate groups
- Ensured unique strong passwords were set and accounts were unlocked

---

## 🧠 6. Group Policy Configuration

- Created GPOs to control logon access:
  - HR GPO: allows `hr-staff` to log on locally
  - IT GPO: allows `it-admins` + remote desktop access
- Applied GPOs to correct OUs using Group Policy Management Console
- Used `gpupdate /force` and `gpresult /r` to confirm application

Optional:
- Set department-specific desktop wallpapers via GPO
- Enabled loopback policy processing (if needed for user-based settings)

---

## 🔐 7. Domain Join & Validation

- Configured Windows 10 clients to use domain controller as DNS
- Joined each client machine to the domain `lab.local`
- Verified each machine appeared in AD under appropriate OU
- Moved computers from `Computers` default container to their department OU

---

## 🔑 8. Logins & Testing

- Logged into each workstation using assigned domain users:
  - SarahB → HR-PC01
  - DanielM → IT-PC01
- Captured screenshots of login screen and desktop
- Validated:
  - Access was granted only to correct users
  - Denied where appropriate
  - GPOs applied as expected

